.Dd July 23, 2026
.Dt XTC_FUTURE 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_future_new_pair ,
.Nm xtc_promise_set ,
.Nm xtc_promise_drop ,
.Nm xtc_future_await ,
.Nm xtc_future_wait ,
.Nm xtc_future_ready ,
.Nm xtc_future_map ,
.Nm xtc_future_then ,
.Nm xtc_future_when_all ,
.Nm xtc_future_when_any ,
.Nm xtc_future_with_timeout
.Nd one-shot awaitable values (futures and promises)
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_future.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_future_new_pair "xtc_promise_t **out_prom" "xtc_future_t **out_fut"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_promise_set "xtc_promise_t *prom" "intptr_t value" "int status"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_promise_drop "xtc_promise_t *prom"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_future_await "xtc_future_t *fut" "intptr_t *out"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_future_wait "xtc_future_t *fut" "intptr_t *out" "int64_t timeout_ns"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_future_ready "xtc_future_t *fut" "int *is_ready"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_future_map "xtc_future_t *src" "xtc_future_map_fn fn" "void *user" "xtc_future_t **out_fut"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_future_then "xtc_future_t *src" "xtc_future_then_fn fn" "void *user" "xtc_future_t **out_fut"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_future_when_all "xtc_future_t **futs" "int n" "xtc_future_t **out_fut"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_future_when_any "xtc_future_t **futs" "int n" "xtc_future_t **out_fut"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_future_with_timeout "xtc_future_t *src" "int64_t timeout_ns" "xtc_future_t **out_fut"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A future is a one-shot awaitable value with a write half
.Pq Vt xtc_promise_t
and a read half
.Pq Vt xtc_future_t .
It is the
.Dq task that returns a value
primitive, distinct from
.Xr xtc_proc 3
(a task with identity and a mailbox): code that needs only one
asynchronous result -- a query answer, a lock grant, a computed value
-- uses a future at near-zero overhead instead of standing up a whole
process.
.Pp
A future carries an
.Vt intptr_t
value and an
.Vt int
status (an
.Dv XTC_OK
or
.Dv XTC_E_*
code).  The promise is set exactly once; the future is then ready
forever and every awaiter observes the same
.Pq value , status .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_future_new_pair
creates a linked pair sharing one reference-counted cell.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_promise_set
completes the promise with
.Fa value
and
.Fa status
and wakes every awaiter; it may be called from any thread.  A promise
is set once -- a second set returns
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL .
Setting consumes the promise handle (do not use or drop it afterward).
.Pp
.Fn xtc_promise_drop
abandons a promise without setting it: the future completes with
.Dv XTC_E_ABORTED ,
so an awaiter never hangs on a broken promise.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_future_await
parks the calling fiber cooperatively until the promise is set, then
resumes and writes the value to
.Fa out
.Pq may be Dv NULL ,
returning the status.  It consumes the future.  It must be called from
within a fiber; off a loop, use
.Fn xtc_future_wait .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_future_wait
is usable from a fiber or a plain OS thread and is bounded by
.Fa timeout_ns
.Pq negative waits forever, 0 polls .
On completion it writes
.Fa out ,
returns the status, and consumes the future; on wait-timeout it returns
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
and does
.Em not
consume the future (it may be awaited again).  A wait-timeout is
distinct from a future that legitimately completed with status
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN :
the former does not consume, the latter does.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_future_ready
sets
.Fa *is_ready
to 1 if the promise is already set, without blocking or consuming.
.Pp
The combinators each consume their input future(s) and return a new
future for the chained result:
.Fn xtc_future_map
transforms the value through
.Fa fn ;
.Fn xtc_future_then
chains to a second future produced by
.Fa fn
when the source completes;
.Fn xtc_future_when_all
completes when all
.Fa n
inputs complete (value is the count; status is the first non-OK
observed, else
.Dv XTC_OK ) ;
.Fn xtc_future_when_any
completes as soon as any one input completes, carrying its result;
.Fn xtc_future_with_timeout
completes with the source's result if it arrives within
.Fa timeout_ns ,
else with
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
.Pq requires a running loop -- it arms a timer .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The
.Ft int
functions return
.Dv XTC_OK
on success,
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
on a bad argument (NULL handle, already-set promise, or
.Fn xtc_future_await
called outside a fiber),
.Dv XTC_E_NOMEM
on allocation failure, or -- for
.Fn xtc_future_await
and
.Fn xtc_future_wait
-- the promise's stored status once completed.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Await a producer's result from within a fiber:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xtc_promise_t *prom;
xtc_future_t  *fut;
xtc_future_new_pair(&prom, &fut);
/* ... hand prom to a producer (another fiber or thread) ... */
intptr_t v;
if (xtc_future_await(fut, &v) == XTC_OK)
	use(v);
.Ed
